@page "/Home"

<PageTitle>WellBore</PageTitle>

<MudText Class="mx-4 px-4 mt-4" Typo="Typo.h2">OSDC WellBore</MudText>
<MudDivider Class="mb-4" />
<MudText Class="mx-6 px-6" Typo="Typo.body1">
    This microservice manages WellBore records: their well and rig associations, sidetrack topology, identity
    assignments, feature assignments, trajectories, survey runs, and portable versioned backups. It persists to
    SQLite, enforces optimistic concurrency on every mutation, and exposes the same operations through a REST API, an
    MCP server, and a Blazor web application, so a wellbore created or edited in one interface is immediately visible
    through the others.
</MudText>

<MudGrid Class="mx-4 px-4 mt-2">
    <MudItem xs="12" md="6">
        <MudPaper Class="pa-6 mt-4" Elevation="2">
            <MudText Typo="Typo.h5" Class="mb-3">What is a WellBore</MudText>
            <MudText Typo="Typo.body1" Class="mb-2">
                A <b>WellBore</b> is the record for one physical drilled path: a name, a description, server-owned
                creation and modification timestamps, an optional <code>WellID</code> linking it to the well it
                belongs to, an optional <code>RigID</code> for the rig that drilled it, its sidetrack topology, and
                the identity and feature assignments that describe it. It is where trajectory geometry, survey
                stations, and casing ultimately attach, and it never stands in for the administrative well record
                itself.
            </MudText>
            <MudText Typo="Typo.body1">
                WellBore UUIDs are caller-generated so that consuming systems can mint and reference an identifier
                before the record exists on the server, which matters when a wellbore is planned before it is
                spudded.
            </MudText>
        </MudPaper>
    </MudItem>
    <MudItem xs="12" md="6">
        <MudPaper Class="pa-6 mt-4" Elevation="2">
            <MudText Typo="Typo.h5" Class="mb-3">WellBores versus wells</MudText>
            <MudText Typo="Typo.body1" Class="mb-2">
                A WellBore is not the same thing as a <b>Well</b>. One Well record covers the entire well — its
                location, identity, and feature record as a planning and administrative unit — while a WellBore is
                one physical drilled path belonging to it, with its own geometry, survey stations, and casing. A well
                is rarely a single hole: it is more often realized as an original bore plus any number of sidetracks,
                each a separate WellBore.
            </MudText>
            <MudText Typo="Typo.body1">
                This is why the two are different microservices: <b>Well</b> owns the location, identity, and feature
                record for the well as a whole, while this <b>WellBore</b> service owns the individual drilled-path
                definitions — the original hole and any sidetracks — each linking back to its Well through the
                optional <code>WellID</code>, an external reference this service validates but never blocks a write
                on.
            </MudText>
        </MudPaper>
    </MudItem>
    <MudItem xs="12" md="6">
        <MudPaper Class="pa-6 mt-4" Elevation="2">
            <MudText Typo="Typo.h5" Class="mb-3">Types of wellbores: original hole and sidetracks</MudText>
            <MudText Typo="Typo.body1" Class="mb-2">
                A wellbore that starts fresh from surface — from a <b>Slot</b> at a Cluster, or as a standalone hole —
                carries <code>IsSidetrack = false</code> and no parent. A wellbore that branches off an existing one
                instead carries <code>IsSidetrack = true</code>, a <code>ParentWellBoreID</code> pointing at the
                wellbore it departs from, and a <code>TieInPointAlongHoleDepth</code> — the along-hole depth in the
                parent where the sidetrack begins, always expressed in SI metres referenced to the WGS84 vertical
                datum.
            </MudText>
            <MudText Typo="Typo.body1">
                Sidetracks are classified — <b>Technical</b> (drilled to bypass an obstruction or fish),
                <b>Production</b>, <b>Appraisal</b>, or <b>Lateral</b> — through an exclusive
                <code>SidetrackClassification</code> feature assignment rather than a dedicated property, so the same
                identity/feature mechanism described below also drives this classification. The legacy
                <code>SidetrackType</code> field is deprecated: the service still accepts it and keeps it
                synchronized with the feature assignment for compatibility, but new clients should read and write the
                feature assignment directly. A parent-child chain of sidetracks can run arbitrarily deep; the service
                rejects cycles and parent links that cross into a different Well when the topology is written.
            </MudText>
        </MudPaper>
    </MudItem>
    <MudItem xs="12" md="6">
        <MudPaper Class="pa-6 mt-4" Elevation="2">
            <MudText Typo="Typo.h5" Class="mb-3">Identities and features</MudText>
            <MudText Typo="Typo.body1" Class="mb-2">
                Every wellbore can carry any number of user-defined <b>identities</b> — symbolic references such as a
                UWI, an operator wellbore name, or a regulatory ID — each with a wellbore-specific value, and any
                number of <b>feature</b> assignments drawn from user-defined categories and options, optionally
                exclusive within a category and optionally time-bounded.
            </MudText>
            <MudText Typo="Typo.body1">
                The identity and feature <i>catalogs</i> are managed on the
                <MudLink Href="/WellBore/webapp/WellBoreIdentities">WellBore Identities</MudLink> and
                <MudLink Href="/WellBore/webapp/WellBoreFeatures">WellBore Features</MudLink> pages, while a specific
                wellbore's assignments are added, replaced, or removed one at a time from the
                <MudLink Href="/WellBore/webapp/WellBore">WellBore</MudLink> page — a definition or option cannot be
                deleted while a wellbore still references it.
            </MudText>
        </MudPaper>
    </MudItem>
</MudGrid>

<MudPaper Class="mx-4 px-4 pa-6 mt-4" Elevation="2">
    <MudText Typo="Typo.h5" Class="mb-3">Contextual data and calculators</MudText>
    <MudText Typo="Typo.body1" Class="mb-2">
        The web application's <b>Contextual Data</b> menu gives read access to the records a wellbore's topology
        relies on: <MudLink Href="/WellBore/webapp/Well">Well</MudLink>,
        <MudLink Href="/WellBore/webapp/Cluster">Cluster</MudLink>, <MudLink Href="/WellBore/webapp/Field">Field</MudLink>,
        and <MudLink Href="/WellBore/webapp/Rig">Rig</MudLink> — each backed by its own microservice and shown here
        through reusable Razor components, so a wellbore can be understood in its well, field, and drilling-program
        context without leaving this application.
    </MudText>
    <MudText Typo="Typo.body1">
        The <b>Calculators</b> menu exposes the same synchronous, stateless evaluations used elsewhere in OSDC —
        <MudLink Href="/WellBore/webapp/FieldCartographicConverter">Cartographic Conversion</MudLink>,
        <MudLink Href="/WellBore/webapp/EarthVerticalDatumCalculation">Vertical Datum Conversion</MudLink>,
        <MudLink Href="/WellBore/webapp/EarthGravityCalculation">Earth Gravity Evaluation</MudLink>, and
        <MudLink Href="/WellBore/webapp/EarthMagneticFieldCalculation">Earth Magnetic Field Evaluation</MudLink> —
        for one-off conversions and lookups while working a wellbore, without switching to another site.
    </MudText>
</MudPaper>

<MudPaper Class="mx-4 px-4 pa-6 mt-4" Elevation="2">
    <MudText Typo="Typo.h5" Class="mb-3">Backup and restore</MudText>
    <MudText Typo="Typo.body1" Class="mb-2">
        Backups are logical, portable JSON, not a raw database copy. From the
        <MudLink Href="/WellBore/webapp/WellBoreBackupRestore">Backup and Restore</MudLink> page, export <b>all</b>
        wellbores or an ordered <b>selection</b> into one versioned document that carries the complete wellbores
        together with only the identity and feature catalog entries they actually reference — enough to reproduce
        the wellbores on another installation without pulling in unrelated catalog clutter. Well, Rig, and
        parent-wellbore UUIDs travel as external references and are not resolved during export.
    </MudText>
    <MudText Typo="Typo.body1">
        Restoring the same document back offers a <b>conflict policy</b> — fail if any wellbore UUID already exists,
        or replace matching wellbores while creating the rest — and a <b>catalog policy</b> — map onto existing local
        identity/feature definitions by UUID or name, or create the missing ones locally. Validation, catalog
        mapping, and every wellbore write happen inside one SQLite transaction, so a restore of many wellbores either
        commits completely or leaves the database untouched.
    </MudText>
</MudPaper>

<MudPaper Class="mx-4 px-4 pa-6 mt-4" Elevation="2">
    <MudText Typo="Typo.h5" Class="mb-3">Data integrity and monitoring</MudText>
    <MudText Typo="Typo.body1" Class="mb-2">
        WellBore updates, deletes, and individual identity/feature assignment changes use <b>optimistic
        concurrency</b>: every mutation must echo back the <code>LastModificationDate</code> from the caller's latest
        read, and a stale request is rejected rather than silently overwriting a concurrent change. Deleting a
        catalog definition or option still in use by a wellbore is likewise rejected, and a wellbore cannot be
        deleted while it still has sidetrack children — the child must be removed or re-parented first.
    </MudText>
    <MudText Typo="Typo.body1">
        Because <code>WellID</code> and <code>RigID</code> point at other services, WellBore also offers read-only
        <b>external-reference validation</b> for one wellbore and a bounded <b>audit</b> across all or selected
        wellbores, each reference reported as <code>Valid</code>, <code>Invalid</code>, or <code>Unavailable</code>
        so a dependency outage is never confused with a genuinely broken reference. The
        <MudLink Href="/WellBore/webapp/StatisticsWellBore">Usage Statistics</MudLink> page reports per-endpoint
        request counts for this service replica.
    </MudText>
</MudPaper>

<MudPaper Class="mx-4 px-4 pa-6 mt-4" Elevation="2">
    <MudText Typo="Typo.h5" Class="mb-3">Using this web application</MudText>
    <MudText Typo="Typo.body1" Class="mb-2">
        Open <MudLink Href="/WellBore/webapp/WellBore">WellBore</MudLink> to browse, search, create, edit, and remove
        wellbores, set their well/rig/sidetrack topology, and manage their identity and feature assignments. Manage
        the shared catalogs on <MudLink Href="/WellBore/webapp/WellBoreIdentities">WellBore Identities</MudLink> and
        <MudLink Href="/WellBore/webapp/WellBoreFeatures">WellBore Features</MudLink>, and move data between
        installations on <MudLink Href="/WellBore/webapp/WellBoreBackupRestore">Backup and Restore</MudLink>.
    </MudText>
    <MudText Typo="Typo.body1">
        The <b>Survey Display</b> menu shows <MudLink Href="/WellBore/webapp/WellBoreTrajectories">WellBore
        Trajectories</MudLink> and <MudLink Href="/WellBore/webapp/WellBoreSurveyRuns">WellBore Survey Runs</MudLink>
        with Rig and mean-sea-level depth-reference integration, and the <b>Contextual Data</b> and
        <b>Calculators</b> menus give access to the related records and evaluations described above. All numeric
        fields follow the unit system selected for the page through the OSDC unit-reference system.
    </MudText>
</MudPaper>

<MudPaper Class="mx-4 px-4 pa-6 mt-4 mb-6" Elevation="2">
    <MudText Typo="Typo.h5" Class="mb-3">Access methods</MudText>
    <MudText Typo="Typo.body1" Class="mb-3">
        The same wellbore data is reachable through several interfaces built on the same underlying store, so it can
        be managed interactively, scripted, embedded in other Blazor applications, or wired into AI agents.
    </MudText>
    <MudSimpleTable>
        <thead>
            <tr><th>Method</th><th>Description</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
            <tr>
                <td><b>Web page</b></td>
                <td>
                    This site — a server-side Blazor application (this page) for browsing, editing, backing up, and
                    restoring wellbores, inspecting contextual data, running calculators, and reviewing usage
                    statistics.
                </td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><b>REST API</b></td>
                <td>
                    JSON endpoints under <code>/WellBore/api</code> for wellbore CRUD, topology and details updates,
                    bounded search, identity and feature catalog management, targeted assignment mutations,
                    external-reference validation and audit, and batch export/restore, documented and explorable
                    through the Swagger UI at <code>/WellBore/api/swagger</code>. Suited to scripts, backend
                    integration, and any HTTP client.
                </td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><b>MCP</b></td>
                <td>
                    A Model Context Protocol server exposed as Streamable HTTP at <code>/WellBore/api/mcp</code> (and
                    WebSocket at <code>/WellBore/api/mcp/ws</code>), publishing 39 tools — every non-statistics REST
                    operation plus connectivity <code>ping</code> — with strict, closed input/output JSON Schemas, so
                    AI agents and MCP-aware tooling can query, create, update, and back up wellbores directly.
                </td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><b>Reusable web pages</b></td>
                <td>
                    The Razor components behind the WellBore, catalog, backup/restore, and survey-display pages are
                    published as the <code>OSDC.Drilling.WellBore.WebPages</code> NuGet package, so other Blazor
                    applications — such as the Well web app — can embed the same unit-aware controls instead of
                    re-implementing this UI.
                </td>
            </tr>
        </tbody>
    </MudSimpleTable>
</MudPaper>
